  "body": "It helps to see your situation as three separate problems stacked on top of each other, because each needs a different response. First, you are having disputes about what agreements meant. Second, your schedules keep shifting, so any fixed arrangement decays quickly. Third, one friend has started avoiding the conversations, which changes what you can realistically do next. The disputes about meaning are probably not mostly bad faith. When people agree verbally about something as ordinary as the bins or the kitchen, they usually each record a different thing: one person hears a promise to do the task tonight, the other hears a vague intention to get to it. Later, both are sincerely sure they are right. So the first practical move is to stop relying on memory: after any verbal agreement, one of you writes it down somewhere all three can see, with the task, the standard (what counts as done), and the deadline. Keep this lightweight. A photo of a note on the fridge or a one-line message in your group chat is enough; the goal is a shared record, not a contract culture. Second, because your schedules change, a static rota will keep breaking. Two things tend to survive schedule churn better than a fixed rota: short recurring check-ins and rotating ownership. Try a standing five-minute weekly check-in at the same time each week, whose only job is to confirm who owns what for the coming seven days and to allow swaps. Give each task a weekly owner rather than a permanent owner, so nobody feels they have been sentenced to the bathroom forever, and add one simple rule: if you cannot do your task that week, you swap in the chat before the deadline, not after. Five minutes of logistics is much easier to attend than an open-ended conversation about feelings, which matters for your third problem. The avoiding friend is the most delicate part. Avoidance in flat-shares usually means the conversations have started to feel punitive, or the person feels the arrangement is already unfair and does not want to defend that view again. So do not open with the chores. Open with the process, and ideally one-to-one and casually: something like, we argue about chores more than any of us wants to, and I think it is because our system is bad rather than because anyone is lazy; can we try a simple one for a month and then decide. Framing it as the system failing rather than a person failing gives the avoidant friend a way back in without conceding blame. It is also worth a gentle private check that nothing bigger is going on: sometimes people withdraw over chores when the real weight is money, hours at work, or feeling taken for granted. On fairness: aim for equal perceived burden rather than equal arithmetic tasks. People with heavier weeks will experience the same task list as heavier, so let people trade tasks they hate for tasks they mind less, and let the weekly check-in absorb the fluctuations. Finally, make any new arrangement explicitly temporary. Agree up front that after four weeks the three of you review it and throw out whatever is not working. This lowers the stakes of trying it and protects you from defending a bad system out of sunk cost. One honest caution: if the arguments quiet down while the system is running, chores were the real issue; if they merely move to another topic, the chores were partly a proxy for something about respect or recognition in the flat, and that would need a different conversation. I do not know your household, and these are patterns that commonly work rather than guarantees; treat the month as the test.",
  "commitments": "Taking this contribution up involves the following. Claim: the disputes about what agreements meant are treated here as symptoms of underspecified verbal agreements rather than bad faith, and this claim could be wrong; if one flatmate is in fact quietly reneging, written records will surface that instead of fixing it, and the advice would need to change. Proposed application: a written record of any chore agreement (task, standard, deadline), a five-minute standing weekly check-in, weekly rotating task ownership with a swap-before-not-after rule, and a private low-stakes reframe offered to the avoiding friend. These are proposed actions, not established solutions; their uptake depends on all three of you, and especially on the avoidant friend accepting at least a low-stakes version, which cannot be assumed. Proposed test and its relevance: run the arrangement for four weeks and compare the frequency and heat of chore disputes against the previous month; the check-in attendance of the avoidant friend is a second observable. Criticism I accept in advance: written systems can read as bureaucratic and can increase resentment in some households, and if a written record starts being used as evidence in arguments, that is a signal to simplify rather than tighten. Unresolved problems I am flagging: the avoidance may be about something other than chores, in which case chore reform will not touch it and a different conversation would be needed; and if arguments persist but move to another subject, the chores were partly a proxy, which this plan does not address. Scope: this is offered for an ordinary flat-share dispute; it assumes no safety concern and no severe breakdown in the friendship, and it should be dropped if either is present. Revision commitment: after the trial, discard what does not fit rather than defending it, and if the written check fails twice to prevent the same dispute twice, treat the disagreement as about standards or fairness rather than memory and renegotiate those directly."
}
